          %p A (locally) noetherian formal scheme is a formal completion of a (locally) noetherian scheme along a closed subscheme. Equivalently, a locally noetherian scheme is a locally ringed space which is locally isomorphic to the formal spectrum of a complete separated adic noetherian ring.
          %h3#more_general_indschemes More general ind-schemes
          %p
            The formal spectrum can be extended to a somewhat bigger class of topological rings than the noetherian ones; Grothendieck developed the theory in the generality of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudocompact+ring"} pseudocompact
            topological rings. However, some important rings, e.g. the ring of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integers"} integers
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ind-schemes"} ind-schemes
            (with at least the requirement that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ind-object"} ind-object
            be represented by a diagram where the connecting morphisms are
            = succeed ");" do
