            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mathbb{Z}[x])/(x^n)
            does.
          %p
            In contrast, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/filtered+colimit"} filtered colimit
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sheaves"} sheaves
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/affine+varieties"} affine varieties
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spec(R)
            that are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} represented
            by this system of nilpotent rings is the formal scheme
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝔸
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ^
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %munder
                      %mi lim
                      %mo ⟶
                    %mi n
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Aff
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi Sh
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi CRing
                    %mi op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\widehat{\mathbb{A}^1}
                  \=
                  \\underset{\longrightarrow}{lim}_n Spec(\mathbb{Z}[x]/(x^{n+1}))
                  \\;\;\;
                  \\in
                  Sh(Aff) = Sh(CRing^{op})
          %p
            given by the sheaf which sends each ring to its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nilradical"} nilradical
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi 𝔸
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ^
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Spec
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi Nil
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\widehat{\mathbb{A}^1} \;\colon\;  Spec(R) \mapsto Nil(R)
                  \\,.
          %p
            (see e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Strickland00"} Strickland 00, example 4.2, example 4.18
          %h3#formal_neighbourhood_of_the_diagonal Formal neighbourhood of the diagonal
          %p
            See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+neighbourhood+of+the+diagonal"} formal neighbourhood of the diagonal
          %h3#formal_groups Formal groups
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+objects"} group objects
            in formal schemes are the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+groups"} formal groups
          %h2#references References
          %p Classical discussion includes
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Grothendieck"}> Alexander Grothendieck
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Géométrie formelle et géométrie algébrique
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Luc+Illusie"}> Luc Illusie
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Grothendieck existence theorem in formal geometry
                %em
                  Fundamental algebraic geometry, Grothendieck’s
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FGA+explained"} FGA explained
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 123
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://cdsagenda5.ictp.it//askArchive.php?categ=a0255&id=a0255s3t3&ifd=15021&down=1&type=lecture_notes"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                A. Grothendieck (avec J. Dieudonne),
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/EGA"} EGA
                I.10
            %li
              %p
                A. Grothendieck et al.
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/SGA"} SGA
                III.2 (Exp. 7a, P. Gabriel, Étude infinitésimale des schémas en groupe et groupes formels; Exp. 7b, P. Gabriel, Groupes formels)
            %li
              %p
                R. Hartshorne,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebraic geometry
            %li
              %p
                M. Demazure, P. Gabriel,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Groupes algebriques
            %li#Demp
              %p
                Michel Demazure, lectures on p-divisible groups
                %a{:href => "http://sites.google.com/site/mtnpdivisblegroupsworkshop/lecture-notes-on-p-divisible-groups"} web
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Murfet"}> Daniel Murfet
                , notes
                %a{:href => "http://therisingsea.org/notes/Section2.9-FormalSchemes.pdf"} Section2.9-FormalSchemes.pdf
            %li
              %p
                Leovigildo Alonso, Ana Jeremias, Marta Perez,
                %em Infinitesimal lifting and Jacobi criterion for smoothness on formal schemes
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0604241"}> arXiv:math/0604241
                )
          %p More general discussion in terms of ind-schemes includes
          %ul
            %li#BeilinsonDrinfeld
              A. Beilinson, V. Drinfel’d,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantization of Hitchin’s integrable system and Hecke eigensheaves on Hitchin system
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.uchicago.edu/~mitya/langlands/hitchin/BD-hitchin.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            Another approach using a certain topological extension of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %msup
                    %mi Alg
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k Alg^{op}
            has been proposed in
          %ul
            %li
              B. Pareigis, R. A. Morris,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Formal groups and Hopf algebras over discrete rings
              %strong 197
              (1974), 113–129 (
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.2307/1996930"}> doi
              ,
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morris-Pareigis+formal+scheme"} nlab entry
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Nikolai+Durov"} Nikolai Durov
            has considered a flexible bigger category (which inludes the usual schemes) of covariant functors from the category of pairs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo ,
                  %mi I
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (R,I)
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a commutative ring and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            a
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/nilpotent+ideal"} nilpotent ideal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebras"} Lie algebras
            based on Hausdorff series is neatly developed and used in that language; see chapters 7–9 of
          %ul
            %li
              N. Durov, S. Meljanac, A. Samsarov, Z. Škoda,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A universal formula for representing Lie algebra generators as formal power series with coefficients in the Weyl algebra
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/j.jalgebra.2006.08.025"} doi:jalgebra
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "http://front.math.ucdavis.edu/math.RT/0604096"} math.RT/0604096
          %p For another generalization of formal schemes see
          %ul
            %li
              T. Yasuda,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Non-adic formal schemes
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1093/imrn/rnp021"} doi:imrn
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0711.0434"} arxiv
          %p
            In a fundamental article in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/noncommutative+algebraic+geometry"} noncommutative algebraic geometry
          %ul
            %li
              M. Kapranov,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Noncommutative geometry based on commutator expansions
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.AG/9802041"} math.AG/9802041
          %p Kapranov introduced objects which should be interpreted as the infinitesimal neighborhoods of those commutative schemes with a closed immersion into a noncommutative scheme which is locally isomorphic to the spectrum of a free associative algebra.
          %p And a decidedly functorial perspective is in (section 4 of)
          %ul
            %li#Strickland00
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Neil+Strickland"}> Neil Strickland
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Formal schemes and formal groups
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.AT/0011121"} math.AT/0011121
          %p
            For the scheme geometric picture behind the infinitesimal neighborhoods and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-modules"} D-modules
            see also
          %ul
            %li
              A. Beĭlinson, J. Bernstein, J.,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A proof of Jantzen conjectures
          %p Some aspects of formal completions from the point of view of the derived categories are in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitri+Orlov"}> D. Orlov
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Formal completions and idempotent completions of triangulated categories of singularities
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0901.1859"} arxiv/0901.1859
            %li
              %p
                Alexander I. Efimov,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Formal completion of a category along a subcategory
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1006.4721"} arxiv/1006.4721
